bonfield ](/samtools/htslib/commits?author=jkbonfield)\n\ncommitted  last week \n\n\u00b7 \n\n34 / 34 \n\n[ c304d28  ](/samtools/htslib/commit/c304d28ea1f7d342379ff97b51919669a90936c7)\n\nCopy full SHA for c304d28 \n\n[ ](/samtools/htslib/blob/c304d28ea1f7d342379ff97b51919669a90936c7/hts.c) [ ](/samtools/htslib/tree/c304d28ea1f7d342379ff97b51919669a90936c7)\n\nMore actions \n\n\n\n\n<::before>\n\n###  Commits on Jul 2, 2026 \n\n  * ####  [ Speed up hts_parse_decimal() handling of oversized exponents ](/samtools/htslib/commit/385f22c0c1b1652168b2a4e5768a88852da9d799 \"Speed up hts_parse_decimal\\(\\) handling of oversized exponents\n\nThe loop to apply exponent values could be made to run for a\nvery long time \\(although not forever\\) if the exponent value\nin the number passed to the function was very large.  As in\nboth the +ve and -ve exponent cases this will eventually\nresult in `n` becoming zero, it's possible to short-cut the\nrest of the loop as the final value will no longer change.\n\\(While the -ve case is obvious, the +ve one is a bit more\ncomplicated due to wrap-around.  However, as each\nmultiplication by 10 adds a factor of 2 \\(as 10 = 5 + 2\\),\nafter at most 64 enough 2's will have accumulated in the\nproduct to make it divide exactly into 2^64 and the result\nwill be zero.\\)\n\nNote that this does not try to add overflow detection, which\nwould require more extensive changes and is left to future\nwork.\n\nSigned-off-by: Rob Davies <rmd+git@sanger.ac.uk>\")\n\nShow description for 385f22c \n\n[ daviesrob ](/samtools/htslib/commits?author=daviesrob)\n\nauthored and \n\n[ whitwham ](/samtools/htslib/commits?author=whitwham)\n\ncommitted  on Jul 2 \n\n\u00b7 \n\n9 / 9 \n\n[ 385f22c  ](/samtools/htslib/commit/385f22c0c1b1652168b2a4e5768a88852da9d799)\n\nCopy full SHA for 385f22c \n\n[ ](/samtools/htslib/blob/385f22c0c1b1652168b2a4e5768a88852da9d799/hts.c) [ ](/samtools/htslib/tree/385f22c0c1b1652168b2a4e5768a88852da9d799)\n\nMore actions \n\n\n\n\n